//? Title: IAF: Parallel Sampling and Sequential Density
//? Subtitle: Recovered-prefix likelihood, strict Gaussian heads and a preserved banana fit
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An inverse autoregressive flow transforms known Gaussian coordinates using
means and log scales that depend on earlier coordinates of that same known
input. This permits a parallel sampling pass. Evaluating the density of an
arbitrary observation instead requires recovering the unknown base coordinates
sequentially. This native C++ study checks both directions on a hand-set strict
conditioner, then fits the original four-stage, 466-value affine teaching model
to procedural banana pools. The complete program keeps all 48 Adam writes,
source masks, actual conditioner and whole-map Jacobians, both roundtrips and
all 1,024 samples. Fixed-final test NLL is 2.879989624 nats per two-coordinate
row, below the fitting-only full Gaussian's 2.994631823, above analytic truth's
2.796480843. This standalone exact-density experiment uses bounded affine
scales; it does not fit a variational posterior or evaluate an ELBO.
)
\toc

> Put the parallel computation in the base-to-observation direction

[Kingma et al., Improving Variational Inference with Inverse Autoregressive
Flow (2016)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.04934) develops autoregressive invertible
transformations for flexible variational posteriors. Our migrated source is a
compact standalone affine density study. It uses the shared degree-masked
Gaussian conditioner rather than the paper's sigmoid-gated posterior update.
For one ordered source block, the known input u produces

$$ x_c=u_c\exp(s_c(u_{<c}))+\mu_c(u_{<c}),\qquad
\log|\det Dg(u)|=\sum_c s_c(u_{<c}). $$
\alttext(Each observation coordinate is an affine transform of its known input coordinate, with heads depending only on earlier known inputs. Its triangular Jacobian has diagonal exp(s) and positive determinant.)

Every u coordinate is supplied, so all heads and x coordinates evaluate in
parallel. Strict own/future exclusion leaves exp(s_c)>0 on the Jacobian's
diagonal. The source uses four 2->16 tanh->4 conditioners with alternating
orders (0,1),(1,0). Heads are mu0,mu1,raw_s0,raw_s1; s=.8tanh(raw_s) bounds
the log scale. The masks are the same as the paired MAF example; which input
is known determines the computational direction.

Given an arbitrary x, the heads require unknown u prefixes. Recover the first
coordinate from its constant heads, recompute on that recovered prefix, then
recover the second:

$$ u_c=(x_c-\mu_c(u_{<c}))\exp(-s_c(u_{<c})),\qquad
\log|\det Dg^{-1}(x)|=-\sum_c s_c(u_{<c}). $$
\alttext(Density evaluation must recover input coordinates sequentially because each later head requires earlier recovered coordinates. The inverse log determinant negates the scales evaluated at the recovered input.)

The source density path reverses blocks3,2,1,0. In each block it makes two
prefix conditioner calls, then **re-evaluates the heads on the fully recovered
input** for the recorded scale/mean fields and negative-sum determinant.
That third call preserves the original graph and floating operation order.
The native API retains it. Its inverse sampling path unscales the supplied
Gaussian coordinates, then applies blocks0,1,2,3 with one parallel call each.
These call counts describe dependency; this study makes no timing claim.

A final positive diagonal density scale gives h=exp(log_scale)×u. Therefore

$$ \log p_X(x)=-\log(2\pi)-\tfrac12\|h\|^2
-\sum_{i=3}^0\sum_{c=0}^1s_{i,c}(u_i)
+\sum_{c=0}^1\mathrm{log\_scale}_c. $$
\alttext(Exact observation likelihood combines Gaussian log density, the four sequential inverse-block negative log-scale sums and the final diagonal scale determinant.)

The loss is negative mean log density in nats per two-coordinate row. All
fitting, sampling banks and epoch selection remain explicit in the report.
Core operations supply reusable maps and owning live differentiable fields.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/iaf_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::Diagram d(28,1150);
 dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;s.width=10;s.height=5;s.font_size=32;
 d.node("split",{-24,0},"Gaussian input u\nUnscale globally",s)
 .node("shift",{-11,0},"Strict heads mu,s\n2 -> 16 tanh -> 4",s)
 .node("add",{2,0},"Parallel sample\nu exp(s) + mu",s)
 .node("stack",{15,0},"4 blocks: 0 to 3\nKnown input heads",s)
 .node("density",{28,0},"Observation x\nDensity: recover u",s)
 .edge("split","shift").edge("shift","add").edge("add","stack").edge("stack","density")
