|---|---:|
| inverse(forward(test)) |4.76837158203e-07|
| forward(inverse(prior)) |7.15255737305e-07|
| Change-of-variables log identity |1.48958863111e-06|
\image(figures/iaf_normalization.svg)
// result_end

\caption(All 1,024 normalization residuals are plotted in original prior-row order, without subsampling. The checks also include bit-exact restore, repeated samples/Jacobians, single/batch parity, per-point determinant cancellation and immutable inference.)
\alttext(1024 blue absolute normalization residuals remain small at the displayed million-fold scale; no row exceeds the original 2e-5-nat tolerance.)

> What the checks establish

All original procedural pools, degree/mask inventories, learned states/partials,
48 Adam writes, validation records, actual conditioner Jacobians, both whole-map
Jacobians, inverse samples, density cells, fitting-only moments and non-timing
metrics match the source exactly. Independent NumPy checks all 22 source archives
(226,631 values), all 144 update captures(44,784 values),17 leaf finite differences
and160 additional masked-coordinate finite differences. It verifies40,704
forbidden conditioner Jacobian entries across recorded evaluations and7,680
zero-gradient/unchanged-weight entries across the 48 writes. It composes both
maps' analytic Jacobian matrices and reconstructs all raw Gaussian/banana rows.

The report preserves the asymmetry of computation: two recovered-prefix calls and one final head re-evaluation
per density block, one parallel call per inverse sampling block. These
counts explain dependency, not a measured speed comparison. The predefined
final test likelihood improves over fitting-only Gaussian baselines while
remaining worse than truth. That result belongs to this 466-value architecture,
retained seeds and short procedural experiment; it is not a tuned best model,
a full-image benchmark or uncertainty estimate across training runs.

